WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.234
♪♪

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.834
-Last "Corrections" of March.

00:00:05.834 --> 00:00:10.601
So, I started
our last "Corrections"

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:13.501
welcoming back Shoemaker,

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:17.767
who had been out
with a leg injury.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.801
I welcomed him back
by singing a few lines

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:23.334
from Eminem's classic song
"Without Me."

00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:26.801
It was pointed out to me
that if I was

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:29.767
true to those lyrics,
I would have said,

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:30.968
"Guess who's back.

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:32.400
Back again.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.033
Shoemaker's back.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.601
Tell a friend.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.567
Guess who's
back, guess who's back."

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.100
I knew that.

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:43.634
I have historically --
While I love that song,

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:47.234
I have had an issue
with those lyrics,

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:52.467
because Eminem tells us,
"Shady's back,"

00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:56.968
but then continues asking us
to guess who's back.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.701
It's a little bit like...

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:02.334
♪ "Guess which hand" ♪

00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:03.801
♪ It's this hand ♪

00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:05.901
♪ Guess which hand,
guess which hand" ♪

00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:15.567
I also was told I did two less

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:19.434
"guess who's backs"
than I should have.

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:24.167
We did make a cut
for the purposes of time.

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:29.067
We are trying to maintain
the internal momentum of

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:32.701
"Corrections," and in doing so,
there are going to be times

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:35.200
where you make an edit
and you lose a

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.367
couple of "guess whose backs,"
just to keep things moving.

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.033
Because there's a real value,
I think, in these

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:47.167
feeling like they're moving
from bit to bit

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:51.534
and not just getting
bogged down in one bit.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:56.767
So, sometimes, you're
going to cut a few bars.

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.334
That's the price.

00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:01.734
Shoemaker is still back.

00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:04.934
First full week having
Shoemaker in the building.

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:07.400
And I will say it

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.267
and I hope it does not
come off too emotional.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.300
The novelty has worn off.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.300
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:20.601
He has his cane.

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:23.234
And the thing about it,
he's using the cane less for

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:27.901
walking and more for, like,
clearing a path.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:30.501
It's a lot of this.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.701
Not endearing.

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:38.767
Someone --
This happens every so often.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:42.067
Someone prefaces
a correction saying --

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:45.834
addressing it
to "whoever -- whatever --"

00:02:45.834 --> 00:02:47.801
excuse me --

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:49.567
"intern is in charge
of reading these."

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.567
No intern.

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:55.701
If I made
an intern read what you wrote,

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:59.000
it would
violate child labor laws.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.234
They're all adults.

00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:06.200
It's just this whole thing
is so inherently childish.

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:13.067
I had a drink with me last week
and I said --

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.200
I described it.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.467
I said it was a gin martini.

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:18.934
There was some outrage.

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:20.334
A lot of you angrily protested

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:25.133
that you never need
to say "gin" before "martini."

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.167
And I agree with that

00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:29.767
It's just with --
I wanted to clarify

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:33.634
it was not a vodka martini,
what with current,

00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:35.734
you know, world events.

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:41.267
I think it's important,
you know,

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:44.067
that people know
the sort of sacrifices...

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.167
Haven't had caviar
in over a month.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.567
Shoemaker keeps telling me
he has a local caviar guy.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:05.801
And I'm not -- I won't say what
happened to the last guy

00:04:05.801 --> 00:04:10.267
who used Shoemaker's
caviar hookup,

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:13.300
but he's survived
by a wife and two kids.

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:18.033
Well, we all -- Oh, you don't
want to laugh at that?

00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:20.033
We all miss Ray.

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:25.501
But he said to me,
on his deathbed,

00:04:25.501 --> 00:04:28.534
after eating
Shoemaker's caviar,

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.367
his last words,

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:33.467
he said,
"Use this in 'Corrections.'"

00:04:39.434 --> 00:04:43.501
Oh, we were once corrected --
This was months ago --

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:46.934
that it was Alice Cooper
bit the head off a chicken,

00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:48.901
Ozzy Osbourne
bit the head off a bat.

00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:50.367
Those aren't the exact details.

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:56.567
But we had gotten the creature
matched with the wrong rocker.

00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:59.801
And I made the assumption
at the time

00:04:59.801 --> 00:05:01.534
that I didn't know much
about that jackal,

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.801
but I will boldly state
that it was a stepdad

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:05.834
who made that correction.

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:08.100
And then we heard a couple
weeks ago

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.100
from that jackal who had,
I guess,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.000
gone back and looked and said,
"I'm happy to tell you that

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.033
that's wrong.

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:15.200
I am a stepmom."

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.467
And we thought that was
super-cool.

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:20.734
And then I, you know,
tried to guess what that

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:24.367
stepmom would say
when she said goodnight.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:27.801
And I went, "Rock 'n' roll."

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:30.367
A bunch of you jumped out and
said this is "rock 'n' roll."

00:05:30.367 --> 00:05:34.334
This is "I love you"
in American Sign Language.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.767
And I know that.

00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:38.434
And it's so crazy to me

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:41.234
that so many of you
do not think

00:05:41.234 --> 00:05:43.834
stepmoms are capable of love.

00:05:48.467 --> 00:05:50.434
And you know whose fault it is.

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:52.901
Disney.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:58.501
Stepmoms call it
"the Disney fallacy."

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:00.300
I'm on some Facebook groups.

00:06:04.501 --> 00:06:06.400
Well, speaking of ASL,
I watched --

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.501
After the Oscars -- I will admit

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.000
it took the Oscars
for me to do it.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.100
I watched "CODA."

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.901
What a wonderful,
wonderful film.

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:17.567
I -- Audible sobbing.

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:20.267
And it is not a depressing film.
It is so uplifting.

00:06:20.267 --> 00:06:22.367
It is such a wonderful film
about family.

00:06:22.367 --> 00:06:26.300
And I think that is the magic
and the value

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.234
of the Oscars is,
you see something Oscars,

00:06:28.234 --> 00:06:30.467
and it makes you go back
and revisit something

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:32.901
that you maybe missed,
which "CODA" was for me.

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:37.167
I also went back and watched
NBC's 2015 limited series

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:38.434
"The Slap."

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:48.167
I should explain that.

00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:49.901
The network reached out
and said that

00:06:49.901 --> 00:06:51.868
"Corrections"
wasn't topical enough.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.701
So, this is a...

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:04.234
The -- I wrote --
So, I wrote a kids' book,

00:07:04.234 --> 00:07:05.601
and one of the things
I've been saying about the

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:07.667
kids' book is -- I'm proud of a
lot of things about it --

00:07:07.667 --> 00:07:10.100
is that
it's the first kids' book --

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.167
the first illustrated kids' book
that has a bear

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:13.567
and a rabbit in it.

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:15.167
And a lot of you were like,
"Oh, there's a bunch."

00:07:15.167 --> 00:07:17.467
But here's a picture
of my book right there.

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:19.100
No, wait.
That's not mine. Hold on.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.133
This is -- There we go.
That's -- Hold on.

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:23.634
This is...

00:07:23.634 --> 00:07:24.734
What are we do-- Guys...

00:07:24.734 --> 00:07:26.934
Hold on. What is -- This is...

00:07:26.934 --> 00:07:29.100
Why -- Who would
put them in this order?

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.934
This is...

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:32.434
Alright, there we are.

00:07:32.434 --> 00:07:36.100
The first -- Who stacked these?

00:07:38.801 --> 00:07:40.200
Oh.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.868
It was Jimmy Reilly.

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:50.501
And if there's a rule at NBC,
it's don't cross a Reilly.

00:07:50.501 --> 00:07:52.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:59.767
Because there's...

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:01.634
there's one around every corner.

00:08:04.067 --> 00:08:07.701
That is -- If you want to know
what the base of power is

00:08:07.701 --> 00:08:10.834
at 30 Rock -- True story.

00:08:10.834 --> 00:08:13.000
I once saw Jimmy Reilly

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.801
pass Lorne Michaels
in the hallway,

00:08:16.801 --> 00:08:20.200
and Jimmy said, "Hey, Lorne."

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.634
And Lorne said,
"Nice to see you, Mr. Reilly.

00:08:30.968 --> 00:08:33.267
I mispronounced
the name of the character

00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:37.534
Saruman -- Saruman
from "Lord of the Rings."

00:08:37.534 --> 00:08:39.033
Saruman. I don't know.

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:41.300
It's so embarrassing to me,
because I did see the

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.734
film, and, obviously,
they say it in the film.

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:45.667
Although, if they didn't say it
in the first 15 minutes...

00:08:45.667 --> 00:08:47.667
[ Snoring ]

00:08:51.667 --> 00:08:54.834
I admitted that I don't get my
tuna from the grocery store,

00:08:54.834 --> 00:08:57.200
but rather the
omakase menu at my

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.734
favorite sushi restaurant.

00:08:59.734 --> 00:09:02.868
A few of you said --
A few of you food snobs

00:09:02.868 --> 00:09:06.000
said there's no
such thing as an omakase menu.

00:09:08.167 --> 00:09:10.033
Omakase is
the absence of a menu.

00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:15.501
It roughly translates
to "I leave it up to you."

00:09:15.501 --> 00:09:17.868
I have been trying the...

00:09:17.868 --> 00:09:23.801
I have been trying
the oMckase menu at McDonald's.

00:09:23.801 --> 00:09:25.400
[ As Johnny Carson ]
Have you heard about this?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.367
Have you heard
about this, Shoemaker,

00:09:31.367 --> 00:09:34.167
the oMckase menu.

00:09:38.601 --> 00:09:40.667
[ Normal voice ] It's only
available at the drive-through.

00:09:40.667 --> 00:09:45.067
It's $50, and they just --
They hand you a bag

00:09:45.067 --> 00:09:47.200
with food of their choosing.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.234
I've done like
eight or nine time --

00:09:48.234 --> 00:09:50.701
Every time,
it's been a Filet-O-Fish.

00:09:50.701 --> 00:09:54.734
Just one,
like, not from that day.

00:09:54.734 --> 00:09:58.200
But it's worth it
for the surprise,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.901
that excitement of
getting a bag,

00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:03.167
and not knowing
until you open it up.

00:10:03.167 --> 00:10:06.100
I mean, it's Filet --

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.767
You kind of know it's
Filet-O-Fish as soon as

00:10:08.767 --> 00:10:10.100
it gets in the car.

00:10:15.567 --> 00:10:17.234
So, I said "light-motif,"
and then I was

00:10:17.234 --> 00:10:18.434
told it's "lei-motif."

00:10:18.434 --> 00:10:19.501
You don't say the "T."

00:10:19.501 --> 00:10:22.934
Whoo! Thank you to
the German viewers

00:10:22.934 --> 00:10:24.234
of "Corrections," who jumped out
and said,

00:10:24.234 --> 00:10:26.400
"That is a German word,
and you say the 'T,'"

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.467
because in Germany,
you say...

00:10:28.467 --> 00:10:30.367
[ German accent ]
...all the letters!

00:10:33.501 --> 00:10:34.601
[ Normal voice ]
Which is better.

00:10:34.601 --> 00:10:37.567
Thank you.
It's not like French words.

00:10:37.567 --> 00:10:40.734
[ French accent ] "Eh,
this is not the 'T' to say."

00:10:43.601 --> 00:10:48.033
Mm...Merch Madness.

00:10:48.033 --> 00:10:49.701
We're down to our
last four mugs.

00:10:49.701 --> 00:10:57.200
You've been voting
@SeaCaptainPolls on Twitter.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:01.701
We're down to our final four,
our final four mugs.

00:11:01.701 --> 00:11:03.367
This was
an underperforming mug

00:11:03.367 --> 00:11:05.334
that didn't even
get out of the final 16.

00:11:05.334 --> 00:11:08.534
Jackéel.

00:11:08.534 --> 00:11:10.868
I feel like this wasn't
appreciated, but...

00:11:10.868 --> 00:11:12.667
I'm going to send it
to Emily Spivey.

00:11:12.667 --> 00:11:15.734
I feel like she'll
still appreciate it.

00:11:15.734 --> 00:11:17.667
So, we're down to our
final four mugs.

00:11:17.667 --> 00:11:18.968
Which mug is going to win?

00:11:18.968 --> 00:11:20.801
Omakase.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.400
Hey, some people have said --
it's very hurtful --

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.334
that this is a rip-off
of Conan O'Brien

00:11:31.334 --> 00:11:33.234
did a section --
a segment, excuse me,

00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:35.133
called "Corrections,"
which is a fantastic bit

00:11:35.133 --> 00:11:36.267
that I love.

00:11:36.267 --> 00:11:37.701
I do feel like
this is different enough,

00:11:37.701 --> 00:11:41.400
but I appreciate that, you know,
it has the same name.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:45.100
And moving forward, you know,

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.968
when we come up with anything,

00:11:46.968 --> 00:11:49.601
we're going to try to avoid
that error in the future.

00:11:49.601 --> 00:11:51.400
So I apologize for that.

00:11:52.701 --> 00:11:55.000
I want to read some comments
we received this week.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.000
I guess you could
call it viewer mail.

00:12:03.734 --> 00:12:05.868
Someone wrote, "I never comment.

00:12:05.868 --> 00:12:10.667
This was the funniest goddamn
thing I've ever watched."

00:12:10.667 --> 00:12:12.901
Asterisk.

00:12:12.901 --> 00:12:14.234
Then you look down.

00:12:14.234 --> 00:12:18.534
Asterisk. "I'm super-high
and kind of drunk right now."

00:12:18.534 --> 00:12:20.300
Just give the compliment.

00:12:22.067 --> 00:12:24.267
This is -- I wanted to
get this word for word.

00:12:26.133 --> 00:12:30.701
"WTF did I just watched
that was horrible."

00:12:31.267 --> 00:12:35.434
Well, I understand someone
like you,

00:12:35.434 --> 00:12:37.400
with no imperfections...

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:40.133
[ Laughter ]

00:12:40.133 --> 00:12:42.667
...wouldn't understand
"Corrections."

00:12:42.667 --> 00:12:44.100
And then someone else --
You know what?

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.968
I'm going to do two nice ones
and one mean one this week.

00:12:45.968 --> 00:12:47.667
Someone wrote,
"Best thing network TV

00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:48.767
has allowed in a while."

00:12:48.767 --> 00:12:50.100
Not technically.

00:12:58.834 --> 00:13:05.734
Oh. Finally, I showed a second
letter from E.B. White

00:13:05.734 --> 00:13:10.234
last week, and there were
a lot of accusations

00:13:10.234 --> 00:13:15.601
about me having
written these letters,

00:13:15.601 --> 00:13:17.267
due to the fact
that there were a litany of

00:13:17.267 --> 00:13:19.868
errors in them
that people feel like

00:13:19.868 --> 00:13:25.133
ol' "Elements of Style"
wouldn't have made.

00:13:25.133 --> 00:13:28.133
You know,
instead of a 1 in the date,

00:13:28.133 --> 00:13:30.934
E.B. used a capital "I."

00:13:30.934 --> 00:13:33.834
He forgot to sign
last week's letter.

00:13:33.834 --> 00:13:36.200
In last week's letter,
he called a key --

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.300
He called a typewriter key
a button,

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:40.868
which a lot of people said
he would never do.

00:13:40.868 --> 00:13:44.434
One person said it couldn't have
been a letter from E.B. White,

00:13:44.434 --> 00:13:45.801
the E.B. White
Collection at Cornell,

00:13:45.801 --> 00:13:48.234
because they would never have
given you the original,

00:13:48.234 --> 00:13:49.467
which I had.

00:13:49.467 --> 00:13:50.767
And I do think -- Looking back,

00:13:50.767 --> 00:13:52.267
I do think
it was a new librarian

00:13:52.267 --> 00:13:55.167
who gave me that,
because when I went.

00:13:55.167 --> 00:13:58.534
back this week to see if there
was another letter,

00:13:58.534 --> 00:14:00.234
she was gone.

00:14:00.234 --> 00:14:03.667
This sort of young, you know,
perky librarian I dealt with.

00:14:03.667 --> 00:14:05.934
Obviously, she messed up and
she did lose her job over it.

00:14:05.934 --> 00:14:10.467
And it was a very --
The woman this week was

00:14:10.467 --> 00:14:15.167
a very officious,
very stern woman,

00:14:15.167 --> 00:14:17.767
what Shoemaker
likes to call a battle-ax.

00:14:22.767 --> 00:14:25.367
And she did -- She gave me
another letter

00:14:25.367 --> 00:14:33.567
and she made it very clear,
the steps I had to take to

00:14:33.567 --> 00:14:34.634
look at a letter like this.

00:14:34.634 --> 00:14:36.767
So, you know, obviously,
to prove this is

00:14:36.767 --> 00:14:40.133
a real E.B. White letter,

00:14:40.133 --> 00:14:43.701
I'm gonna make sure we
get it right,

00:14:43.701 --> 00:14:48.033
because that's what
we're here to do --

00:14:48.033 --> 00:14:49.067
get things right.

00:14:49.067 --> 00:14:52.234
We're not here to mess around,

00:14:52.234 --> 00:14:55.133
especially with something as
historic as a letter like this.

00:14:57.434 --> 00:14:59.100
So we're going
to take our time.

00:15:02.067 --> 00:15:04.100
It's not like
Ben Stiller is here.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:05.834
He is?
[ Laughter ]

00:15:08.501 --> 00:15:10.801
Who was supposed to...

00:15:10.801 --> 00:15:13.734
Oh, Jimmy? Well, that's fine.

00:15:13.734 --> 00:15:15.300
That's fine.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.400
Oh, I wouldn't --
Don't even tell him.

00:16:13.234 --> 00:16:14.434
Okay, here we go.

00:16:14.434 --> 00:16:17.934
Here's the letter --
here's the letter from...

00:16:17.934 --> 00:16:21.000
Let's see here.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.133
Oh, boy. Hold on.
I got to do it this way.

00:16:22.133 --> 00:16:23.400
Here we go.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.767
"Dear Kay, it has been --"

00:16:25.767 --> 00:16:27.133
Oh, first of all,
we see here, because, again

00:16:27.133 --> 00:16:30.968
April 2nd of 1953.

00:16:30.968 --> 00:16:34.567
"Dear Kay, It has been a week.

00:16:34.567 --> 00:16:36.467
I wanted to share
some exciting news.

00:16:36.467 --> 00:16:38.400
I have started a new book
about a pig and a spider.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:41.701
Spoiler alert --
the spider doesn't make it.

00:16:41.701 --> 00:16:43.234
Already laughing
thinking about how hard

00:16:43.234 --> 00:16:44.834
it's going to make kids cry.

00:16:49.067 --> 00:16:51.934
Also found out this week
that typewriters

00:16:51.934 --> 00:16:53.334
have keys and not buttons.

00:16:53.334 --> 00:16:55.901
And, yeah,
I misspelled "typewriters"

00:16:55.901 --> 00:16:57.000
in the previous sentence,

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.634
but I don't have time
to start over.

00:16:59.634 --> 00:17:02.434
I work at 'The New Yorker,'
lest you forget.

00:17:04.701 --> 00:17:06.567
P.S. I'm not signing my
letters anymore,

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:08.968
because, not to be a dick,
my signature is super-valuable,

00:17:08.968 --> 00:17:11.067
and I can't just give it away."

00:17:14.534 --> 00:17:17.667
This is crazy.

00:17:17.667 --> 00:17:20.400
I just noticed -- Hold on.

00:17:22.834 --> 00:17:26.601
This is -- It seems like there's
something else here.

00:17:26.601 --> 00:17:28.467
Can we see this?

00:17:30.534 --> 00:17:33.534
Alright, you guys can see it.
I'm going to read it now.

00:17:33.534 --> 00:17:39.334
"Kay, you must never show
this to anyone.

00:17:39.334 --> 00:17:43.501
I typed it super-small so only
you would see it.

00:17:43.501 --> 00:17:46.667
In my last letter,
I, on purpose,

00:17:46.667 --> 00:17:50.033
wrote "typewriter button"
instead of "key."

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.067
This was a trap.

00:17:57.067 --> 00:17:59.834
If anyone in your life pointed
that out to you

00:17:59.834 --> 00:18:02.400
as a mistake, they are a jackal.

00:18:05.901 --> 00:18:10.534
And you should take all steps
to get them out of your life.

00:18:10.534 --> 00:18:12.734
All the best, E.B. White."

00:18:14.033 --> 00:18:18.400
Whew! What we're learning
about this man.

00:18:23.801 --> 00:18:26.467
Well, I think...

00:18:29.467 --> 00:18:31.834
I think this one
went really well.

00:18:37.968 --> 00:18:39.300
Um...

00:18:42.133 --> 00:18:45.033
I feel like there's
something else.

00:18:45.033 --> 00:18:47.033
But, alternatively...

00:18:50.834 --> 00:18:52.467
there might not be.

